{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CczhGbnXT1D2ZTg2txtNu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"WI-104 is complete and committed. Summary:\n\n## WI-104 — pin the dev toolchain ✅\n\n**The problem (review finding M6):** the kit *runtime* is stdlib-only, but the meta-repo's own verification chain floated on **unpinned** dev tools, so the same declared command ran different machinery per machine — the concrete cost being M9's flaky coverage race on this box's old pytest-cov 4.1.0 vs CI's 7.x.\n\n**What shipped:**\n- **`requirements-dev.txt`** (new, root) — compatible-release (`~=`) pins for `ruff`, `pytest`, `pytest-xdist`, and a **Python-gated `pytest-cov` split** (`~=7.0` on 3.9+, `~=5.0` on the 3.8 floor, since pytest-cov 6.0 dropped 3.8). That per-Python split is the one irreducible difference no pin can erase — and exactly what WI-105 must harden across.\n- **Consumers rewired** to `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt`: both `test.yml` jobs (test + gate) and both meta dev-setup twins (`dev-setup.sh`/`.ps1`).\n- **`canary.yml`** (new) — a **non-gating** weekly job installing *unpinned latest* + running ruff/lint/suite, preserving the early-warning that pinning would otherwise remove.\n\n**Scope held:** kit scripts stay stdlib-only, nothing ships downstream (`project-trajectory/scripts/setup.*` + `ci/check.yml` are the adopter's own templates, untouched). No spine change; no byte-budgeted file touched.\n\n**Verified on the pinned 3.8 resolution** in a throwaway venv (pytest 8.3.5, pytest-cov 5.0.0, ruff 0.15.21): full suite **695 passed / 3 skipped**, ruff format+lint clean, smoke **543 passed**, check_docs/check_trajectory/gen_trajectory --check all clean. Pre-commit floor passed every step.\n\n**A design note worth surfacing:** the pins simply *freeze what CI already installed as \"latest\"* (CI's 3.8 leg already resolved pytest 8.3 / cov 5.0), so the pinned set was already green in CI — only this local box lagged. The pins can't make the 3.8 and 3.9+ legs run identical coverage machinery; that gap is structural and is precisely why WI-105's coverage-plumbing fix must be robust across both.\n\n**State:** `docs/next-wi` → **WI-105** (the coverage-plumbing fix, now verifiable on the pinned toolchain — the review's ranked-first defect); `run-state` RUNNING; not pushed (push-policy: human). The owner queue is unchanged."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1357,"cache_read_input_tokens":127856,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1357},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8da8cf9e-b6b0-4d25-9423-199b55ef91c4","uuid":"df8d4b98-072a-4bb0-925d-05ea1438d2e4","request_id":"req_011CczhGUn6Q661VacNWXLjP"}
